WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.290 --> 00:00:04.140
- Welcome to foundations
of American democracy.

00:00:04.140 --> 00:00:07.370
This is where it all begins.

00:00:07.370 --> 00:00:10.140
You might think it's just
about the United States.

00:00:10.140 --> 00:00:12.350
But here we're going to go much deeper,

00:00:12.350 --> 00:00:14.240
and much further back than that.

00:00:14.240 --> 00:00:16.620
We're gonna go to the original ideas.

00:00:16.620 --> 00:00:18.230
Deep dive into philosophy.

00:00:18.230 --> 00:00:20.200
What are the rights of the individual,

00:00:20.200 --> 00:00:23.600
and what rights are they
willing to give up to a state,

00:00:23.600 --> 00:00:26.210
in order to have those
individual rights protected.

00:00:26.210 --> 00:00:29.240
And this is something that
humanity has struggled with

00:00:29.240 --> 00:00:32.140
for thousands and thousands of years.

00:00:32.140 --> 00:00:35.180
But over those thousands
and thousands of years,

00:00:35.180 --> 00:00:37.790
as for the most part
humanity was controlled

00:00:37.790 --> 00:00:40.180
by kings and emperors,

00:00:40.180 --> 00:00:44.580
little ideas, like democracy,
started to sprout up

00:00:44.580 --> 00:00:47.930
in places like ancient
Greece, and then in Rome,

00:00:47.930 --> 00:00:49.340
and all of these were imperfect,

00:00:49.340 --> 00:00:50.440
and many of us would argue

00:00:50.440 --> 00:00:53.720
that never have they quite
been perfected just yet.

00:00:53.720 --> 00:00:56.010
But really it starts to accelerate

00:00:56.010 --> 00:00:58.780
in this part of our study,

00:00:58.780 --> 00:00:59.670
where we're going to see

00:00:59.670 --> 00:01:01.550
that the enlightenment in Europe

00:01:01.550 --> 00:01:04.910
sprouts these questions
of do we even need kings,

00:01:04.910 --> 00:01:08.070
especially when we just
fought a big war against them.

00:01:08.070 --> 00:01:11.690
Could we create a new type
of governance structure.

00:01:11.690 --> 00:01:13.873
And what you might not realize is

00:01:13.873 --> 00:01:16.460
the United States at its founding

00:01:16.460 --> 00:01:18.460
was really something of an experiment.

00:01:18.460 --> 00:01:22.140
A state, like the United
States, did not exist.

00:01:22.140 --> 00:01:24.750
In fact, in the beginning,
it did not even view itself

00:01:24.750 --> 00:01:28.130
as one state, or one country,
but a collection of countries.

00:01:28.130 --> 00:01:30.420
So as you'll go through this
journey, you're going to see

00:01:30.420 --> 00:01:33.820
this fascinating philosophical deep dive,

00:01:33.820 --> 00:01:38.170
into the ideas of what
makes a government function,

00:01:38.170 --> 00:01:40.250
and what powers should it have,

00:01:40.250 --> 00:01:42.730
and what powers should it not have.

00:01:42.730 --> 00:01:45.820
What powers should go
to the aggregate state,

00:01:45.820 --> 00:01:48.930
the United States, and
what powers should go

00:01:48.930 --> 00:01:52.090
to the individual members,
to the states themselves.

00:01:52.090 --> 00:01:57.090
How does a population protect
themselves from the tyranny

00:01:57.160 --> 00:01:59.770
of a potential king, or
the potential dictator.

00:01:59.770 --> 00:02:02.440
In the coming lessons
we're going to dive deep

00:02:02.440 --> 00:02:05.210
into the ideals of American democracy.

00:02:05.210 --> 00:02:08.400
We're going to learn about the
debates between the groups,

00:02:08.400 --> 00:02:10.740
some of whom thought that
the federal government

00:02:10.740 --> 00:02:13.360
should have more power,
and others who thought

00:02:13.360 --> 00:02:15.350
that the states should have more power.

00:02:15.350 --> 00:02:18.400
And we'll learn how it all played out

00:02:18.400 --> 00:02:20.630
in our actual Constitution.

00:02:20.630 --> 00:02:23.650
What powers are in the hands
of the federal government,

00:02:23.650 --> 00:02:26.170
what powers are in the hands
of the state governments,

00:02:26.170 --> 00:02:28.630
and what powers are in the hands of both.

00:02:28.630 --> 00:02:31.010
So let's start this journey together,

00:02:31.010 --> 00:02:34.580
and to learn more about these ideas,

00:02:34.580 --> 00:02:37.650
and to get practice
with immediate feedback,

00:02:37.650 --> 00:02:41.763
I encourage you to look at these
lessons on KhanAcademy.org.

